<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fifth Pan-African Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Manchester Pan-African Congress, 5th Pan-African Congress</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fifth Pan-African Congress was a meeting of the Pan-African movement held at Chorlton-on-Medlock Town Hall in Manchester, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, from 15 to 21 October 1945. An estimated 87 to 90 delegates from some 50 organisations attended, with more representatives from the African continent than at the four earlier congresses. Among them were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Banda</a>, who later became the first presidents of their independent countries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amy-ashwood-garvey">Amy Ashwood Garvey</a> chaired the opening session and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a> the rest, and the congress demanded independence for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s colonies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fifth-pan-african-congress.jpg" alt="Fifth Pan-African Congress" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KGGucwa · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manchester-fifth-pan-african-conference-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kwame Nkrumah, who had attended the <b>Fifth Pan-African Congress</b> in 1945, returned in 1947 and in 1949 founded the Convention People's Party on a mass following of workers, farmers, ex-servicemen, teachers and market women.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Padmore and the young Kwame Nkrumah organised the <b>Fifth Pan-African Congress</b>, held at Chorlton-on-Medlock Town Hall in Manchester on 15–21 October 1945; Amy Ashwood Garvey chaired its opening session and Du Bois the rest.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
